docs/supervision.md:1:# Independent stage-gate audit
docs/supervision.md:3:For substantive project work, the main agent starts one non-editing `discipline_auditor` alongside scope inspection. The auditor makes an independent acceptance map from the requested scope and, for UI work, the approved render. The main agent then sends the same auditor a compact checkpoint at each applicable gate and waits for a finding **before advancing**. This is an event-driven review of work in progress, not a claim that a second agent sees or controls every tool call. The main agent remains responsible for implementation, browser evidence, fixes, `/review`, and final delivery. Trivial edits use only their relevant checks.
docs/supervision.md:11:| Final closeout | Full requested scope; assembled long-scroll QA for UI work; `MEMORY.md`/`LESSONS.md` updates where warranted; honest delivery claims; no failed or unexamined applicable gate | Consolidated pass/fail/blocked record, artifact paths, final screenshots where applicable, and draft evidence summary |
docs/supervision.md:13:For each handoff, name the gate and changed scope, then give **paths to evidence**, concise command outcomes, and any worker-agent reports. The auditor reads the artifacts, not just the worker's claims. Its reply is `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `BLOCKED`, with the precise discrepancy, evidence location, responsible stage, and next check. An unrun feasible check or implementation defect is `FAIL`; `BLOCKED` is reserved for a concrete external dependency or tool failure preventing the evidence. On `FAIL`, the main agent fixes the issue, reruns the affected checks, and asks the auditor to recheck. On `BLOCKED`, it continues independent work and reports the exact dependency. An auditor's `PASS` cannot turn an absent screenshot, zero-test suite, or unmeasured performance target into proof. Include other subagents' outputs at the applicable checkpoint so their work is covered; the auditor does not automatically receive their transcripts.
docs/supervision.md:15:The auditor profile requests a read-only sandbox and prohibits edits, but a parent session's live sandbox override can supersede that default; verify the effective setting when available and keep the no-edit assignment even if it cannot be enforced. Keep the agent focused on one checkpoint. Keep raw images and logs on disk, send paths and short excerpts, and avoid a second full implementation discussion. If a named custom agent cannot be selected, spawn a subagent with the same non-editing instructions. If subagents are unavailable, perform the same explicit evidence audit in the main thread and report the lost independent check; never imply a second agent ran. The first real site tasks should compare this workflow with the previous one using [agent evals](agent-evals.md): missed gates, material defects at delivery, user corrections, time, and token cost. Keep or adjust the audit cadence based on those results, without waiving the stage gates.
docs/supervision.md:17:## Audit the change log
docs/supervision.md:19:For each applicable gate, require the relevant [CHANGELOG.md](../CHANGELOG.md) IDs alongside artifacts. At scope, check actual/expected and source; at implementation, check mapping/specification and changed-file traceability; at verification and final closeout, check dated technical approval against real evidence and preserve prior failed/reopened events. Missing records, unsupported `VERIFIED` status, or invented user approval are **FAIL**. The auditor does not edit or approve its own implementation; the main agent records the auditor's actual verdict and evidence. Absence of an independent audit on a trivial edit is explicitly `not applicable`, never an invented PASS.
docs/supervision.md:21:## Why this architecture
docs/supervision.md:23:[Codex subagents](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents) support project-scoped profiles and parallel read-heavy work, but each runs its own model/tools and adds cost. Their documented orchestration routes tasks and results through the parent; automatic continuous visibility into every parent and sibling action is not provided by this profile. [Codex hooks](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks) can enforce narrow deterministic conditions, but `agent` hook handlers are currently skipped. A future `Stop` hook should be considered only after a runnable app and concrete, fast gate checks exist; it cannot judge visual fidelity from source code. For a true streaming observer that can steer active turns, a separate application would need to own the [App Server](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app-server) event stream and use `turn/steer` or `turn/interrupt`. That additional controller is unjustified until evidence from agent evals shows the stage auditor is insufficient.
docs/supervision.md:25:## Audit causal learning
docs/discipline.md:1:# Development discipline: one request, complete result
docs/discipline.md:5:Work on a visual slice small enough to compare with the reference, then integrate **every slice in the requested scope** into the full scroll page. A passed slice is an internal milestone, not a handoff. At the start of each slice, make a compact coverage record from the render: content/art, controls and destinations, viewport/locale states, and relevant speed risks. Give each applicable check a result of pass, fail, or blocked with evidence; do not equate “not yet tested” with pass. For every stage, keep the target, observation, defect, fix, and recheck clear. A failed gate routes work back to the responsible stage and reopens downstream checks affected by the change.
docs/discipline.md:9:## Log changes as they happen
docs/discipline.md:11:Follow the required schema and lifecycle in [CHANGELOG.md](../CHANGELOG.md). At scope inspection, search existing IDs; create a record for a new defect/change before implementation, with actual versus expected and source. At implementation, record the cause, mapping/specification → code chain and affected files; use `APPLIED_UNVERIFIED` until checks finish. Record each material failure and reopening immediately under the same ID. At verification, append actual commands/screenshots/results, date, verifier and the applicable auditor verdict. Only evidence-backed technical approval permits `VERIFIED`. Explicit user approval is recorded independently and is never inferred from silence. At delivery, reconcile the current status/index and link remaining open defects. Do this autonomously for small fixes as well as large tasks; use a compact record when appropriate.
docs/discipline.md:15:Keep previous events intact when a conclusion changes. Backfilled records must identify their source and that checks are historical; missing evidence must remain explicit. Reuse the existing topic document for diagnosis, plans and verification; create a separate Markdown document only for a distinct maintained topic or an explicit user request. Keep raw screenshots/logs in the relevant evidence directory. Detailed reports can hold large evidence tables, but the journal must state the defect, solution and approval itself, not merely link to an unexplained report.
docs/discipline.md:23:| 5. Review and optimize | For code changes, invoke native Codex `/review` on the actual diff (CLI equivalent if needed), resolve actionable findings, and inspect architecture, responsibilities, duplication, typing, accessibility, dependencies, and unused code; run relevant static/tests/build checks. Measure the production build: first view, media/JS bytes, LCP/INP/CLS where measurable, network, layout shifts, and long-scroll smoothness. Refactor and improve meaningful problems. | Review findings are resolved or explicitly justified; required checks pass, and the mobile first view remains quick without sacrificing fidelity. An unavailable `/review` is reported, not recorded as passed. | Fix code or delivery; rerun affected static, performance, visual, and behavior checks. |
docs/discipline.md:24:| 6. Learn and report | Analyze every user correction and meaningful self-detected failure; update `LESSONS.md` for reusable prevention and `MEMORY.md` for changed verified state, decisions, or blockers. Complete affected change-log records with technical approval, user-approval state and any open/reopened items. Collect final evidence and limitations. | Persistent project context is current; final response accurately states what passed, what was measured, and what could not be checked. | Do not say done while an applicable gate remains unverified. |
docs/discipline.md:28:For substantive project work, send the [non-editing discipline auditor](supervision.md) the relevant artifacts at each applicable gate, including the final evidence summary. Its independent finding is another checkpoint, not a substitute for executing the gate. If it finds an unrun feasible check, run it before moving on; if it finds a defect, repair and re-audit the affected gate. The auditor also reviews outputs from any other agents at the relevant checkpoint. Keep its handoff concise so independent review does not duplicate the full implementation context.
docs/discipline.md:38:If a check is technically unavailable, record the blocker and completed evidence. Do not silently skip it, replace it with source inspection, or assert an unverified guarantee. See [definition-of-done.md](definition-of-done.md) for the final gate.
AGENTS.md:1:# Ramsider UNO — project rules
AGENTS.md:5:These are the mandatory rules. [docs/discipline.md](docs/discipline.md) defines stage gates and failure routing; [CONTEXT_INDEX.md](CONTEXT_INDEX.md) routes to other details. For substantive project work, read the short [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) for current state, then only the topic files relevant to the task. `PROMPT.txt` and `SUMMARY.txt` are source records for unresolved questions; current user instructions and current assets take precedence over their examples or suggestions.
AGENTS.md:7:## Mandatory change and defect log
AGENTS.md:9:[CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) is the authoritative record of **every logical project change and every discovered defect**, including code, assets, content, mappings, tests, documentation, configuration, user corrections and agent-discovered regressions. The agent maintains it **without a user reminder or a separate permission request**: create/update the entry at discovery, record the solution and changed files when applied, then append verification and approval before delivery. Search relevant IDs when resuming work; do not wait until the final answer to reconstruct the history.
AGENTS.md:11:Use the journal's required fields and lifecycle. Record what was wrong/missing/different, expected result, reference/request, cause, solution, intermediate mapping/specification and how it was applied, changed paths, checks and evidence. Every claimed fix needs a dated **technical approval**, naming the verifier and its evidence (and independent auditor when applicable). `APPLIED_UNVERIFIED` is not `VERIFIED`; blocked/skipped checks cannot supply approval. Explicit user approval is a separate field: never invent it or require it for routine authorized fixes. Reopen a failed fix under the same ID and preserve the previous verdict and failure history. Track remaining defects as open entries, even when outside the current task.
AGENTS.md:15:Small edits may use a short entry, but cannot omit logging or proportionate verification. Group related edits under one logical ID; do not log every tool call. A delivery is incomplete if its journal records or required technical approval are missing. MEMORY describes current state; LESSONS explains reusable prevention; neither replaces CHANGELOG.
AGENTS.md:17:## Work in stages
AGENTS.md:24:6. **Learn and report evidence.** Complete the affected [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) entries with actual checks, technical approval and remaining open IDs. Refresh [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) when verified state, decisions, or blockers changed; record repeatable errors in [LESSONS.md](LESSONS.md). Use [docs/definition-of-done.md](docs/definition-of-done.md). State tested viewports, screenshot comparisons, interactions, build checks, performance measurements, remaining differences, and checks that could not run. Never claim pixel fidelity, WCAG compliance, a 95+ Lighthouse score, or completion without the corresponding evidence.
AGENTS.md:28:For substantive project work, start one independent, non-editing **discipline auditor** alongside scope inspection. Reuse it at each applicable stage gate: scope before implementation; each completed UI slice's rendering and visual evidence; behavior/adaptation; code review and performance; and final integration, memory, and delivery evidence. Give it acceptance checks, changed files, browser images, command results, and any other agents' findings as relevant. Wait for its evidence-based pass/fail/blocked finding before closing a gate. Fix failures and re-audit affected gates; the main agent owns implementation and required checks. Use the project [auditor profile](.codex/agents/discipline_auditor.toml) when available and [supervision protocol](docs/supervision.md); if the profile cannot be selected, give a subagent the same non-editing assignment. Do not spawn it for a trivial edit. The auditor supplements real QA and native `/review`; it cannot certify missing evidence.
AGENTS.md:30:## Standing constraints
AGENTS.md:34:- Working-context efficiency: throughout coding and QA, read only affected code and stage docs; inspect the long reference and screenshots through relevant crops at useful resolution. Keep full-size images, raw image data, long logs, and unrelated file dumps out of model context. Reuse recorded findings, bound tool output, and rerun only checks affected by a change. Never skip required QA to save tokens; stop once all applicable gates pass.

